.TH MACHO-EXPLORER 1 "October 7, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
symbolicate \- resolve the addresses in an Apple crash report to functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho-explorer
.B symbolicate
.I report
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-resolve ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads an Apple crash report and names the function each frame is in, using the
binaries present on this machine.
.PP
Two report formats are supported, and they are read by two separate parsers
because they disagree about the single most important thing in the file:
.RS 4
.TP
.B .ips
The modern format (macOS 12, iOS 15 onward). A one-line JSON header, a newline, then
a JSON payload. A frame carries an \fIoffset into an image\fR, and the image base
must be added to get an address.
.TP
.B .crash
The legacy text format. A frame carries an address that is \fIalready absolute\fR.
.RE
.PP
The format is reported in the result rather than inferred from the file extension,
because the two are routinely saved under each other's extension and a report that
merely changed name did not change what it is. Applying the wrong rule produces
numbers that look like addresses, land inside a real image, and name the wrong
function.
.SH WHY MOST FRAMES STAY UNRESOLVED
Since macOS 11 and iOS 13 the system libraries are not files on disk. They are baked
into the dyld shared cache, so a path such as
.I /usr/lib/system/libsystem_c.dylib
does not exist and there is nothing to read. Most frames in a typical crash log are
in Apple frameworks and therefore cannot be named on the machine that produced the
report.
.PP
This tool reads no shared cache. Instead every frame it cannot resolve carries a
.B reason
naming why, because "this function is not known" and "there was no file to ask" send
a reader to different places. A report that shows a bare
.I 0x...
for both makes you investigate the wrong one. The address and its owning image are
still reported in that case: failing to name a function is not the same as not
knowing where it was.
.SH SOURCES OF A SYMBOL
Each frame carries
.BR symbolSource ,
which distinguishes two claims that look identical in output:
.RS 4
.TP
.B report
The symbol was already in the report. Apple's reporter put it there. This is the
stronger claim and nothing is computed over it.
.TP
.B binary
This package read it out of a file on disk. It is correct for the binary that is
there now, which may not be the build that crashed.
.RE
.PP
The two are counted separately in
.BR totals.fromReport
and
.BR totals.fromBinary .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-arch= name
Prefer one architecture when an image is a universal binary.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-resolve
Parse the report and report each address with its owning image, but read no binaries.
Every frame's
.B reason
says the flag was given, so this is distinguishable from a failure to resolve.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit one JSON object on stdout. Prose goes to stderr.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Ran, and at least one frame resolved.
.TP
.B 1
Ran, and resolved nothing. A negative answer, not an error: the report was read
successfully and there was simply no symbol to be had.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Could not read the report.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
$ macho-explorer symbolicate MyApp-2026-10-07.ips
  MyApp-2026-10-07.ips  —  ips, MyApp, macOS 15.0
  exception EXC_BAD_ACCESS (SIGSEGV)

  Thread 1  (crashed)
     0  -[MyController handleTap:] [binary]
     1  main [report]
     2  (unresolved)
        why not: not on disk — a modern Apple system library lives in the
                 dyld shared cache, so there is no file to read
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR symlookup (1),
.BR describe (1)
